From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam <i@noc.cat>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 09:10:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 68/70] ASoC: qcom: q6apm: don't report an unanswered SPF state
 query

The DSP answers APM_CMD_GET_SPF_STATE only once its signal processing
framework is up. Until then the query goes unanswered, and
q6apm_send_cmd_sync() reports the timeout as an error:

  qcom-apm gprsvc:service:2:1: CMD timeout for [1001021] opcode

That is not an error. No answer means "not ready yet", which
q6apm_is_adsp_ready() reports and q6prm handles by deferring its probe.
Send the query and wait for the answer directly, without the error.
---
 sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm.c b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm.c
index f167b9d..5824455 100644
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6apm.c
@@ -147,7 +147,19 @@ static int q6apm_get_apm_state(struct q6apm *apm)
 	if (IS_ERR(pkt))
 		return PTR_ERR(pkt);
 
-	q6apm_send_cmd_sync(apm, pkt, APM_CMD_RSP_GET_SPF_STATE);
+	/*
+	 * The DSP answers only once its signal processing framework is up. No
+	 * answer means "not ready yet", which the callers poll for, so wait for
+	 * it here without the error q6apm_send_cmd_sync() reports on a timeout.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&apm->lock);
+	apm->state = 0;
+	apm->result.opcode = 0;
+	if (gpr_send_pkt(apm->gdev, pkt) >= 0)
+		wait_event_timeout(apm->wait,
+				   apm->result.opcode == APM_CMD_RSP_GET_SPF_STATE,
+				   5 * HZ);
+	mutex_unlock(&apm->lock);
 
 	return apm->state;
 }
